\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) generalize semantically, but VLM pre-training emphasizes high-level understanding and neglects low-level spatial and dynamic cues needed for precise manipulation. We present UP-VLA, a unified autoregressive VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) on image-text data and future visual prediction (PRE) on robot video data in a single Phi-1.5 backbone. Unified prompting and attention masks mix continuous CLIP-ViT tokens and discrete VQ-GAN tokens, and a joint prediction-and-understanding mechanism conditions action generation on both a generated scene description and predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average completed length of 4.08, a 33\% improvement over GR-1 (3.06), and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D (GR-1: 4.21). On a real Franka Panda, it improves unseen-object grasping and precision tasks. Ablations show that prediction is crucial for spatial grounding and that MMU drives generalization to unseen objects.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large VLMs offer strong semantic grounding and reasoning, and have therefore been used to initialize vision-language-action (VLA) policies for open-world robotics \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla,driess2023palm,li2023vision}. However, VLMs are mostly pre-trained on high-level understanding tasks such as visual question answering, and thus under-emphasize low-level visual and spatial information (depth, size, relative pose) and physical dynamics. These cues are critical for precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes.

Prior work shows a dichotomy (Figure~\ref{fig:fig2}). VLM-initialized VLAs excel at semantic generalization but struggle with fine-grained control. Conversely, prediction-based policies, which pre-train on future frame or video generation \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero,du2023video}, improve spatial grounding and physical consistency but lack semantic robustness. This motivates a unified paradigm that keeps both strengths.

