Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item UP-VLA, a unified VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in an autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention-masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU tokens and discrete predictive tokens in one LLM.
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism conditioning actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art results on CALVIN (ABC$\rightarrow$D average length 4.08, +33\% over GR-1) and real-world gains in both semantic generalization and precision tasks.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}
\textbf{VLM-based VLAs.} RT-2 and related models fine-tune web-scale VLMs to output robot actions \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla,driess2023palm,belkhale2024rt,black2024pi_0}; Robo-Flamingo adapts a VLM into a robot imitator \citep{li2023vision}, and 3D-VLA adds 3D inputs and generative world modeling \citep{zhen20243d}. These inherit semantic generalization from VLM pre-training but are trained mainly on understanding tasks, leaving fine-grained spatial cues under-emphasized.

\textbf{Prediction-based policies.} Another line pre-trains on video or image prediction: GR-1 uses large-scale video generative pre-training \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE edits images into subgoals with diffusion \citep{black2023zero}, and video-language planning predicts future videos \citep{du2023video}; joint-denoising approaches predict visuals together with actions \citep{guo2024prediction}. 3D Diffuser Actor uses diffusion over 3D scene representations \citep{ke20243d}, and Diffusion Policy models actions by diffusion \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy}. These capture dynamics but lack the semantic robustness of VLMs.

\textbf{Unified understanding and generation.} Recent multimodal models unify understanding and image generation in one transformer \citep{xie2024show,dong2023dreamllm,ge2024seed,sun2024autoregressive}. UP-VLA brings this idea to embodied control, with action generation conditioned on both streams.

\section{Preliminaries}
We study language-conditioned manipulation: given the current observation $O_t$ and an instruction $L$, the policy outputs an action sequence $\hat A_{t:t+\Delta t}$. UP-VLA builds on the Phi-1.5 language model \citep{li2023textbooks} and uses two frozen visual pathways: a CLIP-ViT encoder with an MLP projection $E_1$ producing continuous image tokens $u$ for understanding, and a VQ-GAN encoder $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} producing discrete codebook tokens $v$ for prediction, with a VQ decoder reconstructing images. All encoders are frozen; the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

\section{Methodology}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig3_v3.png}
\caption{Overview of UP-VLA.}
\label{fig:fig3}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{Unified prompting and attention masks (Figure~\ref{fig:fig4}).} For MMU the input is $\{[\mathrm{MMU}], u, l\}$: image tokens precede text so they can attend to each other, and the LLM predicts next language tokens, $\hat H_L=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$. For PRE the input is $\{[\mathrm{PRE}], l, v_t\}$: image tokens follow the text and attend to all prior tokens, and future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions, $\hat H_{O_{t+\Delta t}}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4.png}
\caption{Illustration of the unified prompting and attention mechanism.}
\label{fig:fig4}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Joint prediction and understanding for action.} Given $(O_t,L)$, UP-VLA first generates a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ (e.g., ``describe this image''), embeds additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$ into the language space, and forms the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. It then jointly predicts future image tokens and actions, $(\hat H_{O_{t+\Delta t}},\hat H_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L')$. A lightweight policy head (MAP: single-layer attention plus MLP) maps action tokens to continuous actions, $\hat a_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat H_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}))$. Actions thus attend to both semantic (MMU) and predictive visual tokens.

\textbf{Objectives.} $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{MMU}}$ and $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{PRE}}$ are cross-entropy losses over next text tokens and over future image tokens, respectively; $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\|\hat a_{pos}-a_{pos}\|_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat a_{end},a_{end})$. The total loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L=\lambda_1\mathcal L_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal L_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ACT}}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Training.} Stage 1 pre-trains on a mixture of MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k \citep{liu2024visual}) and PRE data (25k Bridge demonstrations \citep{walke2023bridgedata}) with task-specific prompts and masks. Stage 2 fine-tunes actions on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompts, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic capability.

\textbf{Inference.} Given $O_t$ and $L$, the model generates $s$, forms $L'$, runs the PRE pathway to obtain future tokens and action tokens, decodes actions via MAP+MLP, executes, and repeats.

\section{Experiments}
\textbf{Setup.} In simulation we use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D and ABCD$\rightarrow$D settings, reporting the number of tasks completed in a row (out of five) and the average length. In the real world we use a Franka Emika Panda with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, evaluating seen tasks, unseen-object grasping, and precise operations (e.g., cable routing, small-object grasping), with 20 trials per task (Figure~\ref{fig:fig5}). Baselines are VLA-based (Robo-Flamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2, RT-1) and prediction-based (GR-1, SuSIE, Uni-Pi, 3D Diffuser Actor, Diffusion Policy); UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu is a GR-1-like variant with the same backbone but no MMU.

