\textbf{Visualization.} In Figure~\ref{fig:fig7}, UP-VLA identifies objects and approximate relative positions, which helps action grounding, with occasional misidentifications. Predicted futures show robot and object motion consistent with the instruction, with some background color artifacts in unseen scenes (D versus ABC).

\textbf{Limitations.} Generative artifacts (background color shifts) suggest insufficient visual generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. Object misclassification in MMU reflects backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and richer data could reduce it. Joint tasks add training cost and sequence length, which parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory might ease. Extending to 3D inputs, multiple views or explicit physics modeling is future work.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction in a single autoregressive VLA and conditions actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures. It improves both semantic generalization and low-level spatial reasoning, reaching 4.08 (ABC$\rightarrow$D) and 4.42 (ABCD$\rightarrow$D) average length on CALVIN and transferring to real-world precision manipulation.

